M (Identity and Access Management) has introduced a new capability called role manager. This feature automatically sets up IAM roles for AWS services when they are created in the console. Role manager creates or reuses existing roles with the required permissions. Currently, it supports six AWS services, including AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge. When creating an AWS Lambda function, for instance, role manager applies the corresponding AWS-managed template. Roles created via role manager are standard IAM roles that can be controlled by the user, and they can be identified as such in the IAM console. Role manager is available in all AWS Regions except for AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. Users can enable or disable role manager at any time and inspect the AWS-managed templates it deploys. Role manager simplifies the process of setting up IAM roles and allows for easier permission management when ready to tighten security","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-iam-role-manager/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check AWS docs on Amazon Forecast CreateDatasetImportJob S3Config RoleArn cross-account support.", "parent_id": "ea2d5439-3726-4c6a-9ffc-9575a845df1b"}

